<section class="future-v1__section future-v1__resource-hub" aria-labelledby="future-resource-hub-title">
  <div class="future-v1__guide-intro"><p class="future-v1__kicker">Guides &amp; answers</p><h2 id="future-resource-hub-title">Understand the decision before you ask for an estimate.</h2><p>These guides are plain-English starting points for homeowners, builders, and property owners. They explain the questions behind insulation and coating decisions; the conversation about a specific project still depends on the building, access, materials, and site conditions.</p></div>
  <section class="future-v1__guide-framework" aria-labelledby="future-guide-compare-title"><div><p class="future-v1__kicker">How to think about foam types</p><h3 id="future-guide-compare-title">Start with the assembly and the project question.</h3><p>Open-cell and closed-cell foam are different materials. A useful recommendation starts with the space, the surrounding building assembly, available cavity depth, moisture conditions, access, and the outcome you want—not with a universal “best” foam.</p><a class="future-v1__text-link" href="/future/spray-foam-basics#foam-types">Read the foam-types guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div><div class="future-v1__guide-compare-table" aria-label="Open-cell and closed-cell comparison"><div><strong>Open-cell</strong><span>Expands to fill irregular cavities and is often discussed where air sealing is part of the project. Its fit depends on the whole assembly and the location.</span></div><div><strong>Closed-cell</strong><span>Denser and more rigid. It can enter the conversation when cavity depth, assembly conditions, and moisture-management details need closer review.</span></div><div><strong>Ask first</strong><span>What space is involved? What is behind or around it? Is there existing insulation, moisture, access, HVAC, or a renovation plan to consider?</span></div></div></section>
  <section class="future-v1__guide-explainer" aria-labelledby="future-guide-basics-title"><div class="future-v1__guide-diagram" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 500 300"><path d="M44 240h412" /><path d="M105 240V126l145-82 145 82v114" /><path d="M78 105l172-87 172 87" /><path class="future-v1__diagram-fill" d="M132 138h236v102H132z" /><path class="future-v1__diagram-air" d="M60 196h84m210 0h86M247 250V158" /><path class="future-v1__diagram-arrow" d="m132 185 12 11-12 11m224-22-12 11 12 11m-98-61-11 12 11 12" /></svg><span>Heat and air move through the building envelope in different ways.</span></div><div><p class="future-v1__kicker">Spray foam basics</p><h3 id="future-guide-basics-title">Insulation slows heat. Air sealing addresses unwanted air movement.</h3><p>In plain English, insulation helps slow heat moving into or out of a building. Air sealing focuses on gaps, cracks, and connections that can allow uncontrolled air movement. Some foam applications can contribute to both jobs, but the result depends on continuity, the assembly, and the details around penetrations and transitions.</p><div class="future-v1__guide-key-points"><p><strong>Comfort:</strong> Better control of heat flow and unwanted leakage can help a space feel more even.</p><p><strong>HVAC workload:</strong> A more complete thermal and air boundary can reduce heating and cooling load, but each building is different.</p><p><strong>Cost:</strong> Utility savings are not guaranteed. Existing conditions, equipment, weather, occupant use, and the rest of the envelope all matter.</p></div><a class="future-v1__text-link" href="/future/spray-foam-basics">Explore spray foam basics <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></section>
  <section class="future-v1__guide-lowcountry" aria-labelledby="future-guide-lowcountry-title"><div><p class="future-v1__kicker">Lowcountry context</p><h3 id="future-guide-lowcountry-title">Heat, humidity, and the details around attics and crawlspaces.</h3><p>In coastal South Carolina and Georgia, a project may involve hot attics, humid outdoor air, crawlspaces, roof assemblies, and spaces with limited access. That is why the complete building assembly matters. The appropriate approach is determined after reviewing the actual area rather than applying the same material or detail everywhere.</p></div><ul><li>Where does the boundary need to be?</li><li>What insulation is already present?</li><li>What moisture or drainage conditions are visible?</li><li>What ventilation, HVAC, access, and safety details affect the work?</li></ul></section>
  <section class="future-v1__guide-visual-resources" aria-labelledby="future-resource-cards-title"><div class="future-v1__section-heading"><div><p class="future-v1__kicker">Deeper guides</p><h3 id="future-resource-cards-title">Bring a more useful question to the estimate conversation.</h3></div><p>These learning paths go beyond a service overview. They are not promises about a particular property.</p></div><div class="future-v1__guide-card-grid"><a href="/future/spray-foam-basics#foam-types"><span>01</span><h4>Open-cell vs. closed-cell</h4><p>Learn the broad differences and the questions that shape the recommendation.</p><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a><a href="/future/spray-foam-basics#what-to-expect"><span>02</span><h4>What to expect around installation</h4><p>Preparation, access, occupants and pets, and product-specific re-entry guidance.</p><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a><a href="/future/spray-foam-basics#questions"><span>03</span><h4>Questions to ask a contractor</h4><p>Use a simple project checklist before choosing a scope.</p><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a></div></section>
  <section class="future-v1__guide-faq" aria-labelledby="future-guide-faq-title"><p class="future-v1__kicker">Customer FAQ</p><h3 id="future-guide-faq-title">Common questions, answered carefully.</h3><div>{[['Does spray foam stop all air leakage?','It can help air-seal the areas where it is installed and detailed correctly, but a building has many connections and penetrations. The complete air boundary matters.'],['Can spray foam lower heating and cooling costs?','It can reduce heat flow and, where applicable, unwanted air leakage. Actual cost changes vary by the home, systems, weather, energy prices, and use.'],['Can it be installed when it is very hot or cold?','Installation conditions and limits are product- and manufacturer-specific. The installer should follow the selected product’s technical data and actual site conditions.'],['When can people re-enter the area?','Re-entry and ventilation guidance must follow the selected manufacturer’s safety instructions and the specific work conditions.'],['Can old insulation need to be removed first?','Sometimes. Its condition, access, moisture or pest concerns, renovation plans, and the intended system guide that decision.']].map(([question, answer]) => <details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div></section>
  <aside class="future-v1__guide-sources"><strong>Sources / learn more</strong><span>These customer-friendly summaries are informed by public guidance; project decisions remain specific to the building and selected product. Re-entry and ventilation instructions must come from the selected product manufacturer and installer safety plan.</span><div>{sourceLinks.map((source) => <a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.label} <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a>)}</div></aside>
</section>
